<!DOCTYPE node PUBLIC "-//freedesktop//DTD D-BUS Object Introspection 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/dbus/1.0/introspect.dtd">
<!-- Bus: org.legeos.Session. Path: /org/legeos/Session/Lifecycle. Ending, suspending or locking
     the session.

     Before this existed there was no way out of a LegeOS session at all: the session ends when
     lege-comp.service stops (session/lege-session/src/main.rs), and nothing a user could reach
     stopped it. A tester had to switch to a virtual terminal and kill the compositor by hand.

     NOT polkit-gated, and deliberately not in lege-hostd. Every method here forwards to
     org.freedesktop.login1, which already authorises these actions for the active seat through
     its own polkit policy (org.freedesktop.login1.power-off and friends, allow_active=yes). Doing
     it again in LegeOS would mean an administrator prompt to shut down your own laptop, and would
     put a session concern in the privileged daemon that ADR-0003 scopes to administrative
     actions like apt and deployments. sessiond runs unprivileged and needs no new privilege to
     make these calls.

     The Can* methods mirror logind's own so a surface can grey out what is genuinely
     unavailable rather than offering a control that fails on click. Each returns one of
     "yes", "no", "na", "challenge" - logind's own vocabulary, passed through unchanged rather
     than flattened to a boolean, because "challenge" (would need authentication) and "na" (not
     supported by this machine) are different facts and a UI may want to say so. -->
<node>
  <interface name="org.legeos.Session.Lifecycle1">
    <!-- End this session. Stops legeos-session.target through logind's TerminateSession. -->
    <method name="Logout"/>
    <method name="Reboot"/>
    <method name="PowerOff"/>
    <method name="Suspend"/>

    <!-- Lock the session.

         The compositor already implements ext-session-lock-v1 in full
         (compositor/lege-comp/src/session_lock.rs) and ADR-0010 makes the lock screen
         compositor-owned, "one provider by construction". What does not exist yet is anything
         that drives it: no client ever calls lock(), so there is no lock surface to show.

         This method is declared so the interface shape, the CLI verb and the widget region are
         settled, and it currently fails with NotSupported rather than pretending. Making it real
         means giving sessiond a Wayland lock-manager client, which is its own piece of work. -->
    <method name="Lock"/>

    <!-- "yes" | "no" | "na" | "challenge", straight from logind. -->
    <method name="CanReboot">
      <arg name="result" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <method name="CanPowerOff">
      <arg name="result" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <method name="CanSuspend">
      <arg name="result" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
  </interface>
</node>
